        ['Given f(x) = 2x − 3 and g(x) = x² + 1, find (a) f(g(x)), (b) g(f(x)), (c) f⁻¹(x), (d) the value of x for which f(g(x)) = g(f(x)).', ['fg', 'gf', 'Inverse', 'Equation'], '(a) f(g(x)) = 2(x² + 1) − 3 = 2x² − 1. (b) g(f(x)) = (2x − 3)² + 1 = 4x² − 12x + 10. (c) f⁻¹(x) = (x + 3)/2. (d) 2x² − 1 = 4x² − 12x + 10, 2x² − 12x + 11 = 0, x = (6 ± √14)/2.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 10],
        ['Find the domain and range of f(x) = (x − 1)/(x + 2).', ['Domain', 'Range'], 'Domain: all real x except −2. Writing y = (x − 1)/(x + 2) gives x = (2y + 1)/(1 − y), so y ≠ 1; the range is all real y except 1.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['A function f is defined by f(x) = 3x + k and f(2) = 11. Find k and f⁻¹(x).', ['k', 'Inverse'], '3(2) + k = 11, so k = 5. f(x) = 3x + 5, so f⁻¹(x) = (x − 5)/3.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 3, 'theory', 6],
        ['Explain the horizontal line test and use it to show that f(x) = x² has no inverse over the real numbers.', ['Test', 'Parabola', 'Restriction'], 'A function is one-to-one if no horizontal line cuts its graph more than once. The parabola y = x² is cut twice by lines y = k, k > 0, so it is not one-to-one and has no inverse unless the domain is restricted to x ≥ 0.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 4, 'theory', 6],
        ['If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x − 4, show that (f∘g)⁻¹ = g⁻¹∘f⁻¹.', ['fg', 'Inverse', 'Check'], 'f(g(x)) = 2x − 7, so (f∘g)⁻¹(x) = (x + 7)/2. g⁻¹(x) = x + 4 and f⁻¹(x) = (x − 1)/2, so g⁻¹(f⁻¹(x)) = (x − 1)/2 + 4 = (x + 7)/2. They are equal.', 'Compare your answer with the model answer and tick off the points you made.', 5, 'theory', 6],
